WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.434
Mark: I wasn't making a 
comment on your parents.

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.469
I was just saying 
that you go to BU.

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:03.603
I was stating a 
fact, that's all.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.239
Narrator: This is the opening 
scene of \"The Social Network.\"

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:09.142
And this is what that dialogue
looks like in a graph.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:09.976
Mark: You don't
have to study.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.244
Erica: Why do you keep saying
I don't have to study?!

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.712
Mark: Because you go to BU!

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:15.415
Narrator: "The Social Network"
was written by Aaron Sorkin,

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:17.684
a writer whose scripts
are often described

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.953
as "rhythmic" or "musical."

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.588
But those descriptors
are vague,

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:22.655
and I wanted to find out

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:26.159
exactly why Sorkin's writing
sounds so distinctive.

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:27.360
Administrator: I 
don't understand.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.294
Mark: Which part?

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.896
Narrator: So I 
counted every syllable

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:32.298
in each line of dialogue
and put it in a chart.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:36.403
I did my best to pick several
other two-person scenes

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:38.872
with similar attributes
and compared the graphs

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.674
of these scenes to the one
from "The Social Network."

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.210
And what you end up 
with is pretty amazing.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.212
You can actually
see the rhythm

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:49.315
of how Sorkin writes 
compared to other writers.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.885
I also compared this particular
"Social Network" scene

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.653
to other pieces that
Sorkin had written

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.689
and found similar patterns
across his writing.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.459
So, what exactly are 
these rhythmic elements?

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.929
And why does Sorkin incorporate
them in his writing?

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.564
Well, the first 
thing that stands out

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.132
when comparing this
scene to others

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.035
is that Sorkin uses 
longer lines of dialogue.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:11.037
In the opening scene for
"The Social Network,"

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:13.606
Sorkin has nine 
lines of dialogue

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.875
that are over 45
syllables long,

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:18.211
more than twice as many
as are in the opening

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.879
of "When Harry Met Sally,"

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:23.316
one of the film scenes most 
similar to "The Social Network."

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.618
The upside of sprinkling
these mini-speeches

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.654
throughout this scene
is that it creates

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:30.457
more variations in the 
rhythms of the dialogue.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.459
By pairing these
longer lines

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:35.428
with the quick and witty banter
that Sorkin is known for...

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.496
Erica: I'm sorry you're not

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.698
sufficiently impressed
with my education.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:40.500
Mark: And I'm sorry I don't 
have a rowboat, so we're even.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.168
Narrator: He prevents
the exchanges

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.636
from getting too repetitive.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.739
If every line in the scene 
was roughly the same length,

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.675
the exchanges 
would get dull.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.844
You can also see 
just the sheer amount

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.946
of dialogue in 
this one scene.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.648
Sorkin fits considerably
more lines of dialogue

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.450
into this five-minute scene

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.120
than other writers' 
scenes of the same length.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.821
While part of this
is due to the fact

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.356
that Sorkin likes 
to write characters

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:06.226
who naturally speak fast,
he tailors his scripts

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.395
to ensure faster 
dialogue in several ways.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.998
First, he writes 
very few action lines

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.700
compared to other writers.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.302
And Sorkin also controls 
the speed of the dialogue

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.404
by frequently having
characters interrupt

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.706
and speak over each other.

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:20.907
Mark: I'm sorry, I mean it.

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.609
Erica: I appreciate 
that, but I have to go—

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:23.443
Mark: Come on. You 
don't have to study.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:24.677
You don't have to 
study. Let's just talk.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:25.612
Erica: I can't.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:28.114
Narrator: So, why increase
the speed of dialogue?

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.350
Obviously, it's 
entertaining to listen to,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.018
but there's more
to it than that.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.288
Having faster dialogue 
allows for more variation

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:36.756
in the tempo of the scene.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.559
For a good example, take
a look at this clip:

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:40.393
Mark: You don't
have to study.

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:41.528
Erica: Why do you keep saying
I don't have to study?!

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:42.762
Mark: Because you go to BU!

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.665
Narrator: The blurring
exchange of dialogue

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:46.799
between the characters

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.836
increases the impact of
the subsequent pause,

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.338
making the moment 
much more jarring.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.207
The syllable chart 
is great for looking

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:57.010
at the broad patterns of 
rhythm in Sorkin's writing,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.546
but to understand how
he composes his words

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:02.215
to give his dialogue
a musical quality,

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:04.684
I took a closer 
look at his writing.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:06.352
While it is 
natural for words

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.488
to repeat themselves
in dialogue,

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.156
Sorkin has an 
extreme amount of

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.991
repetition in his scripts.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.727
Take a look at the opening
of "The Social Network."

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:15.929
Everything that's
highlighted

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:18.865
is a word or phrase
that's repeated.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:22.368
In fact, out of the 141
sentences in the scene,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:25.505
22 of them are repeated
essentially verbatim.

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:28.908
That's 15% of the 
sentences in the scene.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:31.144
When looking at the 
scenes from other films,

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.646
they have nowhere near
as much repetition.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.748
By listening to these
lines of dialogue,

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.117
you can tell that Sorkin 
is intentionally repeating

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.353
these specific 
words and phrases

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:43.289
to create a rhythm that
simply sounds good.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.657
Erica: I think we 
should just be friends.

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:45.892
Mark: I don't want friends.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:46.826
Erica: I was just
being polite.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.628
I have no intention of 
being friends with you.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:49.462
Mark: I'm under 
some pressure...

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:51.331
Narrator: This repetition
is fun to listen to,

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:53.766
regardless of if you 
pick up on it or not.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:57.070
As Sorkin aptly puts it, 
"What the words sound like

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.240
is as important to me 
as what the words mean."

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.341
If you can believe it,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:03.876
there's actually one
more literary trick

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:06.379
that Sorkin embeds
in his dialogue:

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.281
writing in meter.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.150
Meter is a poetic structure
that was famously used

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.253
by Shakespeare in many 
of his plays and sonnets.

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:16.289
There are several
types of meter—

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:17.924
I won't get into 
the details here—

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.893
but one of the most famous
versions is iambic meter,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.663
which is the repetition
of one unstressed

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.498
and one stressed syllable.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:30.837
Romeo: But soft! What light 
through yonder window breaks?

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.339
Narrator: Because iambic
meter naturally sounds

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:35.942
so appealing, some say it
sounds like a heartbeat.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:37.143
A number of famous lines

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:38.845
from film history 
are written in it.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.980
Gertie: E.T. phone home.

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.084
Elliott: E.T. phone home.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.186
Dorothy: You 
had me at hello.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.822
Maverick: I feel the need...

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:52.558
Both: The need for speed.

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:54.761
Narrator: And some of 
Sorkin's own characters

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:58.064
in "The West Wing" discuss the
merits of writing in meter.

00:04:58.064 --> 00:04:59.565
Toby: He wrote a 
dissenting opinion

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.635
in what I'm almost certain
is trochaic tetrameter.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.303
Narrator: So it should
come to no surprise

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:07.073
that Sorkin incorporates 
meter into his own writing,

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:09.876
especially for some of 
his most memorable lines,

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:11.611
to give his writing 
a little more rhythm

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.146
in important moments.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:14.280
Sean: A million 
dollars isn't cool.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:14.914
You know what's cool?

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.550
Narrator: Using meter is a 
small way to make a sentence

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.519
naturally sound
more pleasing.

00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:21.054
As Sorkin describes it:

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.267
And that's the whole point.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:37.870
The key to why Sorkin's writing
is so exciting to listen to

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.506
is that it literally
sounds like music.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:44.210
Almost any song will 
do, but one song that is

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.146
a particularly good 
comparison to Sorkin's writing

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.382
is "Anything You Can 
Do (I Can Do Better)"

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.584
from the musical 
"Annie Get Your Gun."

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.720
The parallels are
pretty obvious.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.254
No, you can't. Yes, I can.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.122
No, you can't. Yes, I can.

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:58.491
No, you can't. Yes, I can.

00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.393
Erica: There are really
more people in China

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:01.794
with genius IQs than 
the entire population—

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:02.895
Mark: The Phoenix 
is the most diverse.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:03.863
The Fly Club—

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.231
No, you can't. Yes, I can.

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:06.532
No, you can't. Yes, I can.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:07.367
♪ No, you can't ♪

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.234
♪ Yes, I can ♪

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.502
♪ No, you can't ♪

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:10.470
Erica: I have to go study.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.304
Mark: You don't
have to study.

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:12.305
Erica: Why do you keep saying
I don't have to study?!

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:13.506
Mark: Because you go to BU!

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:16.142
Anything you can 
do, I can do better

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.210
Sean: A million 
dollars isn't cool.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:18.044
You know what's cool?

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:18.845
Eduardo: You?

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:21.380
Narrator: Sorkin's
melodic dialogue

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.916
is just plain 
fun to listen to.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:26.386
The rhythm and the 
pulse are engrossing.

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:29.088
At times I don't understand
the technical jargon

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.523
that he uses in
his scripts...

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.191
Mark: They have 10 
minutes to get root access

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.894
to a Python web server, 
expose its SSL encryption,

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:36.896
and then intercept all 
traffic over its secure port.

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.399
Narrator: But it's still
completely entrancing.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:43.002
And this is what I think makes
Sorkin's writing so special.

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:45.805
By putting in the extra 
effort to layer his dialogue

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:48.741
with rhythms and melodies,
he ensures that his scenes

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.244
are incredibly 
entertaining to listen to.

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:54.447
And I would have to agree
with Sorkin when he says:

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:57.416
"It's not just that dialogue
sounds like music to me.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:59.385
It actually is music."

